<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-intro-normal">
  <title>Introduction to the Normal Distribution</title>

  <objectives>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Recognize the shape and defining features of a normal distribution.</p></li>
      <li><p>Identify the two parameters <m>\mu</m> and <m>\sigma</m> from the
      notation <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Describe how changing <m>\mu</m> or <m>\sigma</m> changes the graph.</p></li>
      <li><p>Recognize situations where a normal model is <em>not</em> appropriate.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>The normal distribution is the most important continuous distribution
    in statistics. It is also, as one textbook puts it, the most widely
    abused. Its graph is the familiar bell curve, and you will meet it in
    psychology, business, economics, nursing, the physical sciences, and
    mathematics. Many IQ scores are approximately normal. Real-estate prices
    often are. Some of your instructors may use a normal distribution to help
    determine your grade.</p>

    <p>This chapter studies the normal distribution, the standard normal
    distribution, and their applications.</p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-normal-parameters">
    <title>Two parameters determine everything</title>

    <p>The normal distribution has exactly two parameters<mdash/>two numerical
    descriptive measures: the mean <m>\mu</m> and the standard deviation
    <m>\sigma</m>. If <m>X</m> is a quantity with a normal distribution, we
    write</p>

    <me>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</me>

    <p>Read this aloud as <q><m>X</m> is normally distributed with mean
    <m>\mu</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m>.</q> Those two numbers are
    the whole specification. Nothing else about the distribution is left to
    choose.</p>

    <definition xml:id="def-normal-notation">
      <title>Normal distribution notation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>The notation <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m> means that <m>X</m> is
        normally distributed with mean <m>\mu</m> and standard deviation
        <m>\sigma</m>. For example, <m>X \sim N(170, 6.28)</m> describes a
        normal distribution with mean <m>170</m> and standard deviation
        <m>6.28</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>The probability density function<mdash/>the formula that produces the
    curve<mdash/>is</p>

    <me>f(x) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}
             \, e^{-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{x - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^{2}}</me>

    <p>It is a complicated function. <em>Do not memorize it.</em> You will
    never need to evaluate it by hand. It appears here only so you can see
    that <m>\mu</m> and <m>\sigma</m> are the only quantities in it that
    change from one normal distribution to the next.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 1 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-normal-notation">
      <statement>
        <p>A statistician writes <m>W \sim N(12, 4)</m>. What is the standard
        deviation of <m>W</m>?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>4</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. In the notation <m>N(\mu, \sigma)</m>, the mean comes
            first and the standard deviation second.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>12</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>That is the mean. The second number in the notation is the
            standard deviation.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>2</m>, since <m>\sqrt{4} = 2</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>No square root is needed. The notation lists the standard
            deviation directly, not the variance.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>There is not enough information.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>There is. The two parameters in the notation completely
            determine a normal distribution<mdash/>see
            <xref ref="def-normal-notation"/>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-normal-shape">
    <title>Shape, symmetry, and the effect of the parameters</title>

    <p>The curve is symmetric about a vertical line drawn through the mean
    <m>\mu</m>. Because of that symmetry, the mean and the median of a normal
    distribution are the same number, and both sit at the peak of the
    curve.</p>

    <p>Since the total area under any probability density curve must equal
    <m>1</m>, the two parameters act in different ways.</p>

    <assemblage xml:id="asm-parameter-effects">
      <title>What each parameter controls</title>
      <p>A change in <m>\mu</m> <em>shifts</em> the graph left or right
      without changing its shape.</p>
      <p>A change in <m>\sigma</m> changes the <em>shape</em>: the curve
      becomes fatter and shorter as <m>\sigma</m> grows, skinnier and taller
      as <m>\sigma</m> shrinks. The area stays fixed at <m>1</m> either
      way.</p>
    </assemblage>

    <p>Because <m>\mu</m> and <m>\sigma</m> can be any numbers at all, there
    are infinitely many normal distributions. One of them is special enough to
    get its own name: the <term>standard normal distribution</term>, with
    <m>\mu = 0</m> and <m>\sigma = 1</m>. The next section shows how every
    other normal distribution can be converted into that one.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 2 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-sigma-effect">
      <statement>
        <p>Two normal distributions have the same mean, but the first has
        <m>\sigma = 2</m> and the second has <m>\sigma = 8</m>. How do their
        graphs compare?</p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>They peak at the same place, but the second curve is wider and
            shorter.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Correct. Equal means put the peaks in the same place; the
            larger standard deviation spreads the area out, which forces the
            curve lower.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The second curve is shifted to the right of the first.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Shifting is what a change in <m>\mu</m> does. These two
            distributions have the <em>same</em> mean.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The second curve is wider and also taller, since it covers more
            area.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>Both curves enclose area exactly <m>1</m>. A wider curve must
            therefore be shorter, not taller.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>The graphs are identical, since the mean determines the
            curve.</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>It takes <em>both</em> parameters. Review
            <xref ref="asm-parameter-effects" text="title"/>.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <!-- READING CHECK 3 -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-mean-median">
      <statement correct="yes">
        <p>For any normal distribution, the mean and the median are equal.</p>
      </statement>
      <feedback>
        <p>True. The curve is symmetric about a vertical line through
        <m>\mu</m>, so half the area lies on each side of the mean<mdash/>which
        is exactly what makes it the median as well.</p>
      </feedback>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-normal-limits">
    <title>Where the normal model does not apply</title>

    <p>The normal distribution is extremely useful, but it cannot be applied
    to everything in the real world. A normal model assumes the data is
    symmetric about its center, with values trailing off at the same rate in
    both directions, and with no hard boundary nearby.</p>

    <p>Plenty of real quantities violate this. Household income is strongly
    right-skewed: there is a floor at zero and a long tail of very high
    earners. The time until a lightbulb burns out is skewed as well. The
    number of children in a family is a count, not a continuous measurement,
    and takes only whole-number values. Applying a normal model to data like
    this produces answers that look authoritative and are wrong.</p>

    <!-- READING CHECK 4: short answer, recorded -->
    <exercise xml:id="check-not-normal">
      <statement>
        <p>Think of a quantity you could measure on the students at this
        college that would <em>not</em> be well described by a normal
        distribution. Name it, and in one sentence say what feature of it
        breaks the normal model.</p>
        <response/>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>Many answers work. Some examples:</p>
        <p>Money spent on textbooks this semester: bounded below by zero and
        right-skewed, with most students clustered low and a few spending a
        great deal.</p>
        <p>Number of siblings: a count that takes only whole-number values and
        cannot be negative.</p>
        <p>Hours slept the night before an exam: often bounded above by
        physical limits and skewed by a group of students who barely slept.</p>
        <p>The common features to look for are skew, a hard boundary near the
        bulk of the data, and whole-number-only values.</p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================== -->
  <conclusion>
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>The normal distribution is continuous, bell-shaped, and symmetric about
    its mean. It is specified completely by two parameters: the mean
    <m>\mu</m> and the standard deviation <m>\sigma</m>, written
    <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m>. Changing <m>\mu</m> shifts the graph;
    changing <m>\sigma</m> changes its width. Because the curve is symmetric,
    the mean and median coincide. The special case <m>\mu = 0</m>,
    <m>\sigma = 1</m> is the standard normal distribution, which the next
    section develops.</p>
    <p>Adapted from <pubtitle>Introductory Statistics</pubtitle>
    (OpenStax<nbsp/>/ Lumen Learning), CC BY 4.0.</p>
  </conclusion>
</section>